---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Heading from "../components/Heading.astro";
import { REPO, PACKAGES } from "../consts";

// Factual comparison of approaches. Every row is about mechanism, not
// quality — these are all good tools, and several are far more mature
// than tabnas.
const rows = [
  {
    tool: "tabnas",
    url: null,
    kind: "Runtime rule machine",
    algo: "Deterministic LL, no backtracking",
    grammar: "Data: ABNF, JSON, or API",
    codegen: "None",
    extend: "Add rules to an existing grammar",
    self: true,
  },
  {
    tool: "ANTLR",
    url: "https://www.antlr.org",
    kind: "Parser generator",
    algo: "ALL(*)",
    grammar: ".g4 file",
    codegen: "Generates source",
    extend: "Edit the grammar, regenerate",
  },
  {
    tool: "Peggy (PEG.js)",
    url: "https://peggyjs.org",
    kind: "Parser generator",
    algo: "PEG, ordered choice + backtracking",
    grammar: ".peggy file",
    codegen: "Generates source",
    extend: "Fork the grammar",
  },
  {
    tool: "Chevrotain",
    url: "https://chevrotain.io",
    kind: "Parser toolkit",
    algo: "LL(k), configurable lookahead",
    grammar: "TypeScript methods",
    codegen: "None",
    extend: "Subclass the parser",
  },
  {
    tool: "nearley",
    url: "https://nearley.js.org",
    kind: "Parser generator",
    algo: "Earley: any CFG, ambiguity allowed",
    grammar: ".ne file",
    codegen: "Generates source",
    extend: "Edit the grammar, recompile",
  },
  {
    tool: "tree-sitter",
    url: "https://tree-sitter.github.io",
    kind: "Parser generator",
    algo: "GLR, incremental",
    grammar: "grammar.js",
    codegen: "Generates C",
    extend: "inherits / injection, then regenerate",
  },
];
---

<Base
  wide
  title="Other parsers"
  description="How tabnas differs, mechanically, from ANTLR, Peggy, Chevrotain, nearley, tree-sitter, and a hand-written parser."
>
  <article class="cmp">
    <Heading level={1} id="tabnas-and-other-parsers">tabnas and other parsers</Heading>
    <p class="lede">
        Compare the grammar representation, parsing algorithm, and tooling
        each project provides. Follow the project links for its own
        documentation and supported features.
      </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tool</th>
            <th>Kind</th>
            <th>Algorithm</th>
            <th>Grammar lives in</th>
            <th>Code generation</th>
            <th>Extending someone else's grammar</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.self ? "self" : ""}>
              <th scope="row">
                {r.url ? <a href={r.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r.tool}</a> : r.tool}
              </th>
              <td>{r.kind}</td>
              <td>{r.algo}</td>
              <td>{r.grammar}</td>
              <td>{r.codegen}</td>
              <td>{r.extend}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="the-one-real-difference">Grammar representation</Heading>
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.antlr.org/">ANTLR</a> and
        <a href="https://peggyjs.org/">Peggy</a> generate parser code from
        grammar definitions. tabnas executes a rule table at runtime, so
        plugins can change the grammar without generating parser source.
      </p>
      <p>
        tabnas keeps the grammar as data the engine walks at runtime. That
        makes extension the normal case rather than a fork: you add rules and
        token alternates to a grammar that already exists, which is how
        <a href={REPO("jsonc")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">jsonc</a> is built on
        <a href={REPO("json")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">json</a>, and
        <a href={REPO("jsonic")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">jsonic</a> on that.
        It also makes the grammar inspectable while it runs: you can print it
        back as ABNF or draw it. <a href="/why/">Why tabnas</a> has the longer version.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://chevrotain.io/docs/">Chevrotain</a> also runs without
        code generation. Its grammar uses a JavaScript DSL; tabnas exposes
        a rule table that can be defined as data or through an API.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent can produce ABNF or a declarative tabnas rule table. Neither
        representation establishes that the grammar is correct: validate it
        and test valid and invalid inputs. The <a href="/agents/">agent
        guide</a> describes those checks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="when-to-use-something-else">When to use something else</Heading>
      <dl>
        <div>
          <dt>Your grammar is ambiguous</dt>
          <dd>
            tabnas is deterministic and does not backtrack. If you need
            every valid parse of an ambiguous input,
            <a href="https://nearley.js.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nearley</a>'s
            Earley parser is built for that; PEG's ordered choice with
            backtracking handles a different set of awkward cases.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>You're building editor tooling</dt>
          <dd>
            <a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tree-sitter</a>
            reparses incrementally on every keystroke and recovers from errors
            mid-file. tabnas does neither, and isn't trying to.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>You need a mature grammar for a big language</dt>
          <dd>
            ANTLR's
            <a href="https://github.com/antlr/grammars-v4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">grammars-v4</a>
            has hundreds of battle-tested grammars. tabnas has
            {PACKAGES.length} packages and one maintainer.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>You're working in a language the engine doesn't run in</dt>
          <dd>
            The engine is implemented in TypeScript and Go. ANTLR generates
            for Java, C#, Python, Go, JavaScript, C++, Swift and more.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>You just need to parse JSON</dt>
          <dd>Use your platform's JSON parser. Really.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="what-tabnas-asks-of-you">What tabnas asks of you</Heading>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You write the lexer.</strong> tabnas draws a hard line between
          lexing and parsing and is mostly about the parsing half. It gives you
          tools for the lexing part, but you assemble it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Left recursion is a rewrite, not native support.</strong> The
          ABNF compiler rewrites left-recursive rules (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left_recursion#Removing_all_left_recursion"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paull's algorithm</a>), so the
          resulting tree is flat rather than left-nested, and a purely
          left-recursive rule with no base branch is an error.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It's pre-1.0.</strong> Minor versions can break things, and
          grammar packages track the engine release they were built against.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine">
        If something on this page is wrong or unfair to one of these projects,
        please <a href={`${REPO("parser")}/issues`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">say so</a>: getting a comparison wrong is worse than not having one.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .cmp { max-width: 60rem; }
  .cmp :global(h1) { font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3rem); margin: 0 0 1rem; text-wrap: balance; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 62ch; }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 2rem 0 0; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.66rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--ink-mute);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 700; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody td { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  tr.self th, tr.self td {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-strong) 12%, transparent);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  section { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .cmp :global(h2) { font-size: 1.6rem; margin: 0 0 0.85rem; }
  section > p { color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 66ch; }

  dl { margin: 1rem 0 0; }
  dl > div { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  dt { font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
  dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 64ch; }

  ul { color: var(--ink-soft); padding-left: 1.2rem; max-width: 66ch; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; }
  li strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .fine { font-size: 0.95rem; }
</style>
